{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A1WzuivDHPiXuj9Yx4X8NX","type":"tool_result","content":"\"\"\"Why cover excess two? The ridge against the thresholds of the random bipartite incidence graph.\n\nQuestion (`reports/ml_nature_plan_3.md` §1 Q3, loop0004 item 09). §25 found\nthe hardness ridge -- the density at which the nodes to refute `optimum - 1`\npeak at fixed `n` -- at cover excess `(n_ones - m) / n ~ 2-2.4` for every\nproduct ratio `m / n` from 2 to 1/8, i.e. at about one independent cycle of\nthe customer-product incidence graph per customer (its cyclomatic number is\n`n_ones - n - m + c`). Why two? This module treats the excess as a quantity\nof the random bipartite incidence graph and asks whether any of that graph's\nknown thresholds coincides with the ridge:\n\n  * the giant component / 2-core emergence (branching factor 1; Schmidt-Pruzan\n    & Shamir 1985, Karonski & Luczak 2002 for hypergraphs; Molloy-Reed);\n  * the emergence of the deeper cores of the bipartite graph -- the\n    (3, 2)-core (customers with >= 3 products, products with >= 2 customers),\n    the (2, 3)-core and the (3, 3)-core -- which appear discontinuously, as\n    the k-core of G(n, p) does for k >= 3 (Pittel, Spencer & Wormald 1996;\n    Fernholz & Ramachandran 2007; Riordan 2008; Molloy 2005 for hypergraphs);\n  * natural values of the 2-core's own quantities: half the customers in the\n    core, the core's cyclomatic number equal to its customer count (the\n    XORSAT-style condition, Dubois & Mandler 2002, Mezard, Ricci-Tersenghi &\n    Zecchina 2003, where the 2-core of the factor graph has as many\n    constraints as variables), the core's excess two;\n  * the random-intersection-graph literature's thresholds for G(n, m, p) at\n    m = Theta(n) (Karonski, Scheinerman & Singer-Cohen 1999; Behrisch 2007;\n    Lageras & Lindholm 2008; Rybarczyk 2011): the giant is the branching\n    factor above; connectivity sits at products per customer ~ ln n, which\n    moves with n where the ridge does not.\n\nEvery threshold is derived in the configuration model with the generators'\nactual degree distributions (Poisson products per customer with the empty-row\nrepair; products of exactly `d` customers for the fixed generator, Poisson for\nBernoulli) in `col_mean` coordinates, checked against peeled cores on the\nregenerated instances of every cell at n in {50, 60, 75}, and then scored\nagainst §25's measured peaks two ways: at its natural value (an uncalibrated\nprediction of where the ridge sits per `m / n`) and calibrated at `m = n` as\n§25 (d) did. The empirical core, kernel and dominance-reduced quantities are\nadded to §25's constancy table.\n\nNothing here is a bound, a solver change or a prediction of a value; nothing\nis written to `solutions/`. Instances regenerate byte for byte from the\nensemble manifests.\n\nUsage:\n    python -m learning.cover_excess --stage measure --workers 16   # peel every certified instance at n in {50, 60, 75}: ~2 min\n    python -m learning.cover_excess --stage tables                 # reports/cover_excess_tables.md, ~1 min\n    python -m pytest tests/test_cover_excess.py -q\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport time\nfrom concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom scipy.stats import binom, poisson\n\nfrom learning.ensemble import ENSEMBLE_DIR, Cell\nlearning/excess.py:82:def excess(n: float, m: float, n_ones: float) -> float:\nlearning/excess.py:87:def giant_threshold_excess(r: float, generator: str = \"fixed\") -> float:\nlearning/excess.py:103:def ratio_label(r: float) -> str:\nlearning/excess.py:115:def incidence_components(matrix: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\nlearning/excess.py:125:def incidence_stats(matrix: np.ndarray) -> dict:\nlearning/excess.py:162:def collection_of(source_file: str, instance_name: str) -> tuple[str, str]:\nlearning/excess.py:180:def solution_record(instance: MOSPInstance, solutions_dir: Path = SOLUTIONS_DIR) -> tuple[float, str]:\nlearning/excess.py:195:def _corpus_job(args) -> dict:\nlearning/excess.py:206:def corpus_rows(workers: int) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:218:def _ensemble_job(args) -> dict:\nlearning/excess.py:235:def ensemble_jobs(ensemble_dir: Path = ENSEMBLE_DIR) -> list[tuple]:\nlearning/excess.py:255:def ensemble_rows(workers: int, ensemble_dir: Path = ENSEMBLE_DIR) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:262:def _sim_job(args) -> dict:\nlearning/excess.py:280:def simulation_rows(workers: int, sim_csv: Path = SIM_CSV) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:290:def _map(fn, jobs, workers: int) -> list:\nlearning/excess.py:297:def default_workers(cap: int = 8) -> int:\nlearning/excess.py:303:def build(workers: int, out: Path = OUT_CSV) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:326:def load(path: Path = OUT_CSV) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:337:def _quantile_row(group: pd.DataFrame) -> dict:\nlearning/excess.py:355:def collection_table(frame: pd.DataFrame) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:369:            def _key(c):\nlearning/excess.py:378:def series_table(frame: pd.DataFrame, source: str = \"ensemble\") -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:403:def repaired_giant_threshold_excess(generator: str, r: float) -> float:\nlearning/excess.py:414:def crossing_table(frame: pd.DataFrame, min_n: int = CHECK_MIN_N, tol: float = CHECK_TOLERANCE) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:459:def bound_gap_table(frame: pd.DataFrame) -> tuple[pd.DataFrame, pd.DataFrame]:\nlearning/excess.py:499:def forest_regime_table(frame: pd.DataFrame) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:525:def lines_table(frame: pd.DataFrame) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:542:def _md(frame: pd.DataFrame, floatfmt: str = \".3g\") -> str:\nlearning/excess.py:546:def tables(frame: pd.DataFrame) -> str:\nlearning/excess.py:577:def figure(frame: pd.DataFrame, out: Path = FIGURE, min_n: int = CHECK_MIN_N) -> Path:\nlearning/excess.py:646:def sizes_text(matrix: np.ndarray) -> str:\nlearning/excess.py:653:def sizes_from_text(text: str) -> dict[int, int]:\nlearning/excess.py:662:def _rule_on(instance: MOSPInstance) -> tuple[int, float]:\nlearning/excess.py:669:def _rule_ensemble_job(args) -> dict:\nlearning/excess.py:682:def _rule_corpus_job(args) -> dict:\nlearning/excess.py:697:def rule_rows(workers: int, out: Path = RULE_CSV, ensemble_dir: Path = ENSEMBLE_DIR) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:720:def load_rule(path: Path = RULE_CSV) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:726:def _ols(x: np.ndarray, y: np.ndarray) -> tuple[float, float, float, float]:\nlearning/excess.py:740:def series_key(frame: pd.DataFrame) -> pd.Series:\nlearning/excess.py:747:def series_slopes(rule: pd.DataFrame, min_n: int = SLOPE_MIN_N, min_sizes: int = 3) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:797:def matched_pairs(slopes: pd.DataFrame, tol: float = PAIR_TOLERANCE) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:825:def rule_by_bin(rule: pd.DataFrame) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:842:def rule_worst(rule: pd.DataFrame, top: int = 10) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:858:def named_groups(slopes: pd.DataFrame) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:879:def beta_residuals(slopes: pd.DataFrame) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:903:def slopes_tables(rule: pd.DataFrame) -> str:\nlearning/excess.py:941:def figure_opt(rule: pd.DataFrame, out: Path = FIGURE_OPT) -> Path:\nlearning/excess.py:998:def main() -> None:\nlearning/cover_excess.py:77:def degree_pmfs(generator: str, r: float, c: float, repair: bool = True,\nlearning/cover_excess.py:120:def size_biased(pmf: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\nlearning/cover_excess.py:129:def _tail_ge(pmf_other: np.ndarray, prob: float, k_min: int) -> float:\nlearning/cover_excess.py:137:def core_fixed_point(p_c: np.ndarray, p_p: np.ndarray, k_c: int, k_p: int,\nlearning/cover_excess.py:158:def core_quantities(generator: str, r: float, c: float, k_c: int = 2, k_p: int = 2,\nlearning/cover_excess.py:186:def core_threshold_col_mean(generator: str, r: float, k_c: int, k_p: int, c_lo: float = 1.0,\nlearning/cover_excess.py:190:    def empty(c: float) -> bool:\nlearning/cover_excess.py:206:def connectivity_col_mean(r: float, n: int) -> float:\nlearning/cover_excess.py:212:def invert_analytic(fn, target: float, c_lo: float = 1.0001, c_hi: float = 400.0, iters: int = 22,\nlearning/cover_excess.py:241:def _q(key: str, k_c: int = 2, k_p: int = 2):\nlearning/cover_excess.py:263:def threshold_table(peaks: pd.DataFrame, min_n: int = 50) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/cover_excess.py:296:def threshold_summary(table: pd.DataFrame) -> pd.DataFrame:\n# The Chu & Stuckey generator, the first-moment bound under it, and the threshold against the ridge\n\n*2026-10-31, agent report. Everything here lives in `reports/scratch/clique_cover_moment/`; nothing\noutside it was touched, nothing committed. All runs under `nice -n 19`, at most 8 processes.*\n\nScripts: `stats_cs.py` (the 200 files), `gen_candidates.py` + `fit_generators.py` + `fit_variants.py`\n+ `diag_hist.py` (Q1), `bound_lib.py` + `mc_empty_pairs.py` + `floor_violation.py` (Q2),\n`q3_thresholds.py` (Q3). Logs beside each. Extracted paper texts in `lit/`.\n\n## 0. Orientation and a correction to the README\n\nThe files are `m n` then an `m × n` matrix, **rows = patterns, columns = pieces = customers**\n(Frinhani's `Dataset_Description`; the repository's solver reads them that way, and the certified\noptima agree with Frinhani's S1 table, e.g. Random-100-50-2 mean 11.6). `Random-n1-n2-d`: n1 =\ncustomers, n2 = products (Chu & Stuckey list \"number of customers, number of products\"; the\nfile for `Random-100-50` has 50 rows of 100 columns).\n\n**The README's \"every customer has at least two patterns\" is the wrong way round.** Over the 200\nfiles the *pattern* (row) minimum is 2 in every class and the *customer* (column) minimum is 1 in\nmost classes (5 of 5 files at every d = 2 class, 4–5 of 5 at d = 4, 1–5 at d = 6; none at d ≥ 8 for\nthe square classes). So: every pattern has at least two customers; customers with a single pattern\nare common (17–23 % of customers at d = 2, 48 % in Random-100-50-2).\n\n## 1. What the generator is\n\n### 1.1 What the papers say\n\nChu & Stuckey (2009), §4.2, p. 253 of LNCS 5732 (`lit/chu_stuckey_2009.txt` lines 534–549), verbatim:\n\n> First we specify the number of customers, number of products and the average number of\n> customers per product. We then calculate a density that will achieve the specified average\n> number of customers per product. The customer vs product table is then randomly generated\n> using the calculated density to determine when to put 1's and 0's. As a post condition, we\n> throw away any instance where the customer graph can be decomposed into separate components.\n\nChu's PhD thesis (2011), §6.4.2, pp. 151–152, repeats this word for word. Neither mentions a\nminimum pattern size or any repair of empty customers. Frinhani et al. (2018)'s dataset\ndescription only says \"sets of five instances with 2, 4, 6, 8 and 10 pieces per pattern were\ncreated by a pseudorandom instance generator\" and that no instance is a complete graph, tree or\n1-tree. No held paper gives the code.\n36:Chu's PhD thesis (2011), §6.4.2, pp. 151–152, repeats this word for word. Neither mentions a\n42:### 1.2 The joint statistics (`stats_cs.log`)\n117:(acceptance < 1/2,000 at (75,75,2) already). So the customer repair is needed, and it is the only\n181:| (30,30,6) | best fit (Bernoulli, pad C≥1, pad P≥2, connected) | t=2: **0.996** ±0.007; t=3: **0.978** ±0.027; t=4: **1.082** ±0.132 |\n235:| (30,30,2) | 1000 | 7.78 | 4.95 | 3.64 | 0.0000 | **0.0000** | 1 | 142 s |\n236:| (30,30,4) | 1000 | 15.81 | 12.86 | 11.24 | 0.0000 | **0.0000** | 2 | 194 s |\n253:true optimum in 98–100 % of instances; the 0.1–2 % below it at d ≥ 6 are what \"bound < 1\"\n273:g(1/2) = ln 2 + μ ln(2·2^{−d} − 0) = ln 2 · (1 + μ(1 − d)). So g(1/2) < 0 iff\n280:depend on the size distribution's shape, only on its mean, because at a = 1/2 the per-pattern\n281:term ln(2(1−a)^s − (1−2a)^s) = ln 2 − s ln 2 is linear in s; it does depend on quenched (sizes","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"44e8c6d7-9d93-4ce6-b6cb-7af364193448","uuid":"9b83b485-ae23-43f9-98a6-a396330f9c16","timestamp":"2026-11-01T00:12:24.536Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\"\"\"Why cover excess two? The ridge against the thresholds of the random bipartite incidence graph.\n\nQuestion (`reports/ml_nature_plan_3.md` §1 Q3, loop0004 item 09). §25 found\nthe hardness ridge -- the density at which the nodes to refute `optimum - 1`\npeak at fixed `n` -- at cover excess `(n_ones - m) / n ~ 2-2.4` for every\nproduct ratio `m / n` from 2 to 1/8, i.e. at about one independent cycle of\nthe customer-product incidence graph per customer (its cyclomatic number is\n`n_ones - n - m + c`). Why two? This module treats the excess as a quantity\nof the random bipartite incidence graph and asks whether any of that graph's\nknown thresholds coincides with the ridge:\n\n  * the giant component / 2-core emergence (branching factor 1; Schmidt-Pruzan\n    & Shamir 1985, Karonski & Luczak 2002 for hypergraphs; Molloy-Reed);\n  * the emergence of the deeper cores of the bipartite graph -- the\n    (3, 2)-core (customers with >= 3 products, products with >= 2 customers),\n    the (2, 3)-core and the (3, 3)-core -- which appear discontinuously, as\n    the k-core of G(n, p) does for k >= 3 (Pittel, Spencer & Wormald 1996;\n    Fernholz & Ramachandran 2007; Riordan 2008; Molloy 2005 for hypergraphs);\n  * natural values of the 2-core's own quantities: half the customers in the\n    core, the core's cyclomatic number equal to its customer count (the\n    XORSAT-style condition, Dubois & Mandler 2002, Mezard, Ricci-Tersenghi &\n    Zecchina 2003, where the 2-core of the factor graph has as many\n    constraints as variables), the core's excess two;\n  * the random-intersection-graph literature's thresholds for G(n, m, p) at\n    m = Theta(n) (Karonski, Scheinerman & Singer-Cohen 1999; Behrisch 2007;\n    Lageras & Lindholm 2008; Rybarczyk 2011): the giant is the branching\n    factor above; connectivity sits at products per customer ~ ln n, which\n    moves with n where the ridge does not.\n\nEvery threshold is derived in the configuration model with the generators'\nactual degree distributions (Poisson products per customer with the empty-row\nrepair; products of exactly `d` customers for the fixed generator, Poisson for\nBernoulli) in `col_mean` coordinates, checked against peeled cores on the\nregenerated instances of every cell at n in {50, 60, 75}, and then scored\nagainst §25's measured peaks two ways: at its natural value (an uncalibrated\nprediction of where the ridge sits per `m / n`) and calibrated at `m = n` as\n§25 (d) did. The empirical core, kernel and dominance-reduced quantities are\nadded to §25's constancy table.\n\nNothing here is a bound, a solver change or a prediction of a value; nothing\nis written to `solutions/`. Instances regenerate byte for byte from the\nensemble manifests.\n\nUsage:\n    python -m learning.cover_excess --stage measure --workers 16   # peel every certified instance at n in {50, 60, 75}: ~2 min\n    python -m learning.cover_excess --stage tables                 # reports/cover_excess_tables.md, ~1 min\n    python -m pytest tests/test_cover_excess.py -q\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport time\nfrom concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom scipy.stats import binom, poisson\n\nfrom learning.ensemble import ENSEMBLE_DIR, Cell\nlearning/excess.py:82:def excess(n: float, m: float, n_ones: float) -> float:\nlearning/excess.py:87:def giant_threshold_excess(r: float, generator: str = \"fixed\") -> float:\nlearning/excess.py:103:def ratio_label(r: float) -> str:\nlearning/excess.py:115:def incidence_components(matrix: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\nlearning/excess.py:125:def incidence_stats(matrix: np.ndarray) -> dict:\nlearning/excess.py:162:def collection_of(source_file: str, instance_name: str) -> tuple[str, str]:\nlearning/excess.py:180:def solution_record(instance: MOSPInstance, solutions_dir: Path = SOLUTIONS_DIR) -> tuple[float, str]:\nlearning/excess.py:195:def _corpus_job(args) -> dict:\nlearning/excess.py:206:def corpus_rows(workers: int) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:218:def _ensemble_job(args) -> dict:\nlearning/excess.py:235:def ensemble_jobs(ensemble_dir: Path = ENSEMBLE_DIR) -> list[tuple]:\nlearning/excess.py:255:def ensemble_rows(workers: int, ensemble_dir: Path = ENSEMBLE_DIR) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:262:def _sim_job(args) -> dict:\nlearning/excess.py:280:def simulation_rows(workers: int, sim_csv: Path = SIM_CSV) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:290:def _map(fn, jobs, workers: int) -> list:\nlearning/excess.py:297:def default_workers(cap: int = 8) -> int:\nlearning/excess.py:303:def build(workers: int, out: Path = OUT_CSV) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:326:def load(path: Path = OUT_CSV) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:337:def _quantile_row(group: pd.DataFrame) -> dict:\nlearning/excess.py:355:def collection_table(frame: pd.DataFrame) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:369:            def _key(c):\nlearning/excess.py:378:def series_table(frame: pd.DataFrame, source: str = \"ensemble\") -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:403:def repaired_giant_threshold_excess(generator: str, r: float) -> float:\nlearning/excess.py:414:def crossing_table(frame: pd.DataFrame, min_n: int = CHECK_MIN_N, tol: float = CHECK_TOLERANCE) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:459:def bound_gap_table(frame: pd.DataFrame) -> tuple[pd.DataFrame, pd.DataFrame]:\nlearning/excess.py:499:def forest_regime_table(frame: pd.DataFrame) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/excess.py:525:def lines_table(frame: pd.DataFrame) -> pd.DataFrame:\nlearning/