{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UBDy1zvJ15RxCQasLvqAT6","type":"tool_result","content":"paper1/ijoc_literature.md:19:interval graph, interval thickness, pebbling, register allocation, consecutive\npaper1/ijoc_literature.md:28:ordering, chordal, exact algorithm graph, isomorphism symmetry, symmetry\npaper1/ijoc_literature.md:64:pebbling, register allocation, interval graphs, consecutive ones; dominance,\nreports/random_graph_pathwidth_survey.md:448:Gogate–Dechter's finite-`n` treewidths sit inside every interval, and they fall\nreports/random_graph_pathwidth_survey.md:632:answer lies in our proved interval, and on 120 graphs where both are exact the\n64:* `branch_lemma` — **the pathwidth branch lemma** (Fellows & Langston 1987,\n65-  Lemma 4.3; Kinnersley 1992, Corollary 4.2 and the remark before Theorem 4.3):\n66-  three pairwise disjoint *connected* vertex sets of pathwidth `≥ k`, each\n67-  *adjacent to* a vertex `v` outside them, force `pathwidth ≥ k + 1`.\n68:  `branch_lemma_treewidth` is the same with the branches' treewidth. The proof\n69-  is the interval argument: in a decomposition of width `k` each branch fills a\n70-  bag of its own (`exists_bag_subset_of_le_pathwidth_induce`); `v` avoids the\n71-  middle one, and the branch on the far side of it is joined to `v` on the near\n72-  side and to its own bag on the far side, so being connected it crosses the\n73-  middle bag (`exists_mem_bag_of_connected`, `middle_bag_false`). No hypothesis\n74-  on edges *between* the branches is needed.\n75:* `branch_lemma_separator` — **Lemma A** of `reports/ml_nature.md` §38 (loop0004\n76-  item 13, 2026-09-28), the separator form: the cut vertex is replaced by a set `S`\n77-  disjoint from the branches such that every two branches are joined through a\n78-  connected subset of `S` (a component of `G[S]`, in §38's words). Proved through\n79:  `branch_lemma_linked`, where each pair of branches has its own connector, any\n80-  connected set disjoint from the third branch and adjacent to both; the middle-bag\n81-  step (`middle_bag_false_linked`) needs no case on which side the cut vertex sits,\n82-  since one of the two outer branches or the connector must cross the middle bag.\n83:  `branch_lemma_of_separator` re-derives `branch_lemma` as the case `S = {v}`;\n84:  `branch_lemma_separator_treewidth` is the treewidth-seeded form item 11's\n85-  `sep-branch` candidate computes.\n86-* `old_branch_statement_false` — the statement this file carried with `sorry`\n87-  until 2026-09-28 asked only that the branches be pairwise non-adjacent,\n88-  neither connected nor attached to `v`, and **was false**: four isolated\n89-  vertices, `v = 0`, branches `{1}`, `{2}`, `{3}`, `k = 0`, pathwidth `0`\n90-  (`pathwidth_bot_fin4`). The literature's forms carry both hypotheses: the\n91-  branches are the components of `G − v` (hence connected) that `v` is\n92-  attached to.\n93-\n94-## The fitted candidate (proved 2026-10-31; nothing left stated without proof)\n95-\n96-* `minClosedNeighborhood G t` — `f(t)`, the smallest closed neighbourhood of a\n--\n751:theorem branch_lemma (v : V) (A B D : Finset V) (k : ℕ)\n752-    (hvA : v ∉ A) (hvB : v ∉ B) (hvD : v ∉ D)\n753-    (hAB : Disjoint A B) (hAD : Disjoint A D) (hBD : Disjoint B D)\n754-    (hconnA : (G.induce (↑A : Set V)).Connected) (hconnB : (G.induce (↑B : Set V)).Connected)\n755-    (hconnD : (G.induce (↑D : Set V)).Connected)\n756-    (hadjA : ∃ a ∈ A, G.Adj v a) (hadjB : ∃ b ∈ B, G.Adj v b) (hadjD : ∃ d ∈ D, G.Adj v d)\n757-    (hA : k ≤ pathwidth (G.induce (↑A : Set V))) (hB : k ≤ pathwidth (G.induce (↑B : Set V)))\n758-    (hD : k ≤ pathwidth (G.induce (↑D : Set V))) :\n759-    k + 1 ≤ pathwidth G := by\n760-  have hne : (Set.range (fun P : PathDecomposition G => P.width)).Nonempty :=\n761-    ⟨_, PathDecomposition.trivial G, rfl⟩\n762-  obtain ⟨P, hP⟩ := Nat.sInf_mem hne\n763-  change P.width = pathwidth G at hP\n--\n795:theorem branch_lemma_treewidth