{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018gHCUnF4UEz1r12V8rCLgY","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"pathwidth of block graphs polynomial algorithm chordal graphs NP-hard Gustedt\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"The Neighborhood Polynomial of Chordal Graphs\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2008.08349\"},{\"title\":\"Technical Report 221-1989\",\"url\":\"https://page.math.tu-berlin.de/~combi/coga-old/publications/techreports/1989/Report-221-1989.html\"},{\"title\":\"Pathwidth is NP-Hard for Weighted Trees\",\"url\":\"https://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-00462314\"},{\"title\":\"2009 | OriginalPaper | Buchkapitel\",\"url\":\"https://www.springerprofessional.de/pathwidth-is-np-hard-for-weighted-trees/3115376\"},{\"title\":\"# Treewidth and Small Separators for Graphs with Small Chordality*\",\"url\":\"https://ics-archive.science.uu.nl/research/techreps/repo/CS-1995/1995-02.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"link.springer.com\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/doi/10.1007/3-540-52846-6_99\"},{\"title\":\"people.engr.tamu.edu\",\"url\":\"https://people.engr.tamu.edu/j-chen3/iwpec2009/images/12.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9802025\"},{\"title\":\"treewidth ktrees chordal\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.uoi.gr/~loukas/courses/grad/Algorithmic_Graph_Theory/index.files/treewidth-ktrees-chordal.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"134054 the complexity of minimum length path decompositions\",\"url\":\"https://pub.pg.edu.pl/en/download/134054-the-complexity-of-minimum-length-path-decompositions\"}]\n\nThe search turned up Gustedt's work on chordal graph pathwidth, but nothing that settles block graphs specifically.\n\n- **Gustedt (1989/1993):** His technical report \"On the Pathwidth of Chordal Graphs,\" published in DISAM vol. 45 (1993), is listed with an abstract. It states that the path width problem for chordal graphs is NP-hard. It then gives polynomial algorithms for two subclasses to clarify the structural reasons for the intractability. Those subclasses are k-starlike graphs, a generalization of split graphs, and primitive starlike graphs, where the intersection behaviour of maximal cliques is strongly restricted.\n- **Related hardness:** Mihai and Todinca also showed that the pathwidth problem is NP-hard for particular subclasses of chordal graphs, and that this carries over to weighted trees. A separate source says pathwidth remains NP-complete on chordal graphs, planar graphs and graphs with bounded maximum degree.\n- **Block graphs:** None of the results directly addresses the pathwidth of block graphs. The closest hit is a bandwidth paper on a special class of block graphs, which is a different parameter. Checking Gustedt's full text or a survey on pathwidth of block graphs would be needed to confirm whether block graphs are polynomial or NP-hard.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"44e8c6d7-9d93-4ce6-b6cb-7af364193448","uuid":"75480d08-a1dd-4075-a3b2-400781172cc2","timestamp":"2026-11-01T00:16:26.734Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"pathwidth of block graphs polynomial algorithm chordal graphs NP-hard Gustedt","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01W7S1nox9ftBTSC2stts3ES","content":[{"title":"The Neighborhood Polynomial of Chordal Graphs","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2008.08349"},{"title":"Technical Report 221-1989","url":"https://page.math.tu-berlin.de/~combi/coga-old/publications/techreports/1989/Report-221-1989.html"},{"title":"Pathwidth is NP-Hard for Weighted Trees","url":"https://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-00462314"},{"title":"2009 | OriginalPaper | Buchkapitel","url":"https://www.springerprofessional.de/pathwidth-is-np-hard-for-weighted-trees/3115376"},{"title":"# Treewidth and Small Separators for Graphs with Small Chordality*","url":"https://ics-archive.science.uu.nl/research/techreps/repo/CS-1995/1995-02.pdf"},{"title":"link.springer.com","url":"https://link.springer.com/doi/10.1007/3-540-52846-6_99"},{"title":"people.engr.tamu.edu","url":"https://people.engr.tamu.edu/j-chen3/iwpec2009/images/12.pdf"},{"title":"arxiv.org","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9802025"},{"title":"treewidth ktrees chordal","url":"https://www.cs.uoi.gr/~loukas/courses/grad/Algorithmic_Graph_Theory/index.files/treewidth-ktrees-chordal.pdf"},{"title":"134054 the complexity of minimum length path decompositions","url":"https://pub.pg.edu.pl/en/download/134054-the-complexity-of-minimum-length-path-decompositions"}]},"The search turned up Gustedt's work on chordal graph pathwidth, but nothing that settles block graphs specifically.\n\n- **Gustedt (1989/1993):** His technical report \"On the Pathwidth of Chordal Graphs,\" published in DISAM vol. 45 (1993), is listed with an abstract. It states that the path width problem for chordal graphs is NP-hard. It then gives polynomial algorithms for two subclasses to clarify the structural reasons for the intractability. Those subclasses are k-starlike graphs, a generalization of split graphs, and primitive starlike graphs, where the intersection behaviour of maximal cliques is strongly restricted.\n- **Related hardness:** Mihai and Todinca also showed that the pathwidth problem is NP-hard for particular subclasses of chordal graphs, and that this carries over to weighted trees. A separate source says pathwidth remains NP-complete on chordal graphs, planar graphs and graphs with bounded maximum degree.\n- **Block graphs:** None of the results directly addresses the pathwidth of block graphs. The closest hit is a bandwidth paper on a special class of block graphs, which is a different parameter. Checking Gustedt's full text or a survey on pathwidth of block graphs would be needed to confirm whether block graphs are polynomial or NP-hard."],"durationSeconds":5.592431073999993,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018gHCUnF4UEz1r12V8rCLgY","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","