/scratch/forest_regime/fit.log   # every table below\npython -m pytest tests/test_block_trees.py tests/test_chou_block.py -q\n```\n\n### 3.1 Findings\n\n1. **The literature has the exact answer, and it is polynomial.** Neither\n   held survey (`reports/random_graph_pathwidth_survey.md`,\n   `paper1/ijoc_literature.md`) mentions block graphs. OpenAlex and a web\n   search do: Chou, Ko, Ho & Chen, *Node-searching problem on block graphs*,\n   Discrete Applied Mathematics 156 (2008) 55–75,\n   doi:10.1016/j.dam.2007.08.007, answer the question of Peng et al. (whether\n   node searching on block graphs is polynomial) with an\n   `O(c² + b c + n)` algorithm (`c` cut vertices, `b` blocks; their Theorem\n   26) built on **Theorem 4**: for a block graph and `k ≥ 2`, `ns(G) ≤ k` iff\n   (`VC_k`) every vertex has at most two branches with `ns ≥ k` and (`BC_k`)\n   every block `K` has an edge `(x, y)` whose *enclosure subgraph*\n   `{G}_{x,y}` — `K − x` together with the non-block branches at the vertices\n   of `K` other than `x` and `y` — has `ns < k`. `VC_k` alone is the tree\n   rule of Ellis, Sudborough & Turner (1994); `BC_k` is what the cliques\n   add, and `ns = pathwidth + 1`. Pathwidth is NP-hard on chordal graphs\n   (Gustedt 1993), of which block graphs are a subclass. **We found no prior\n   statement** of a closed form on clique trees; the closed form below is\n   ours and is a conjecture.\n2. **The kill criterion is met: no `α (ω − 1) + β h + γ` fits the grid.** Of\n   the 325 forms with `α, β ∈ {0..4}`, `γ ∈ {−6..6}`, none is exact on the 29\n   values; the best (`2 (ω − 1) + h − 2`) misses 14. Even one form per `b`\n   misses 2–3 of its 8–11 values. The reason is in the differences: at\n   fixed `(h, b)` the value grows in `s` by `2, 1, 2` at `h = 1`, `2, 2, 2`\n   at `h = 2` and `3, 2` at `h = 3` — the slope in `ω` **rises with the\n   depth** (3/2, 2, 5/2), which no form linear in `ω` and `h` can do.\n3. **The values are multiplicative in `ω` and the depth, with the tree's own\n   pathwidth added.** Every one of the 29 grid values, and every exact\n   extension value, equals\n\n       C(s, h, b) = ⌈(h + 2)(s − 2) / 2⌉ + pw(CT(2, h, b)),\n\n   with `pw(CT(2, h, b))` read from the `s = 2` row (1 for `b = 1`;\n   1, 2, 2 for `b = 2` at `h = 1, 2, 3`; `h` for `b = 3`). So each level of\n   depth costs `(ω − 2) / 2`, the clique floor `ω − 1` is the `h = 0` case,\n   and the branching `b` enters only through the tree. At `h = 1` this is\n   `⌈3ω/2⌉ − 2` for every `b`, the same value the caterpillar of cliques\n   takes at every length (1, 3, 4, 6 for `s = 2..5`): a clique whose `s − 2`\n   other vertices each carry a clique of the same size forces half of those\n   cliques through its own bag, which is the `BC_k` condition of finding 1\n   at work. `C` is a conjecture on this family, checked on every value\n   solved (table 3.5); the extension's censored cells are consistent with it\n   (their intervals contain it).\n4. **The additive candidates are not upper bounds; only the multiplicative\n   ones are, and they are loose.** On the grid, `ω − 1 + pw(B)` is violated\n   once (`CT(5, 2, 2)`: 7 against 8), `ω − 2 + pw(B)` five times (worst by\n   2), `ω − 1 + h` seven times (worst by 2); the blow-up bound\n   `ω (pw(B) + 1) − 1` and the TreeBound form are never violated and never\n   exact, with mean slack 7.4 and 9.9 on the grid, 5.5 and 10.3 on the\n   caterpillars, 17 and 25 on the random block trees. The ratio of the\n   blow-up bound to the exact value on the grid is 1.9–5. The caterpillar is\n   the case the item named as discriminating, and there the additive\n   `ω − 2 + pw(B)` is exact on all 24 and `ω − 1 + pw(B)` is one above on\n   the 18 with `s ≥ 3`; it is the clique trees with `h ≥ 2`, not the\n   caterpillars, that separate additive from multiplicative.\n5. **Off the structured family the pathwidth is the largest pattern.** On\n   the 3,448 forests on record `pw = ω − 1` on 3,442 (99.8%; the six\n   exceptions are one above, all Bernoulli instances with `ω ≤ 5` and\n   `pw(B) ≤ 3`); on the 898 random block trees solved `pw ∈ {ω − 1, ω}`,\n   `ω − 1` on 833 (92.8%) and `ω` on 65, never more, at `ω` means 4.6–8.8\n   and `pw(B) ≤ 3`. The random size law never builds the nested structure\n   of finding 3: a block tree needs `s − 2` loaded vertices on one clique,\n   repeated down a path of cliques, to climb above `ω − 1`, and random\n   attachment at a uniform customer spreads the load. So in the forest\n   regime of the map (`e < e_g(r)`, where 74–92% of the ensemble instances\n   are forests), the MOSP optimum is the largest pattern on all but a few\n   per thousand instances, and `max(ω − 1, ω − 2 + pw(B))` is never below\n   the truth there (0 violations on 4,346 random and record instances).\n6. **The search is the wrong tool for block graphs, and the record's forest\n   values were free.** Two random block trees at `n = 120` (`d = 2`, index\n   8, and `d = 3`, index 13) were censored at 600 s after `2.2 × 10⁹` and\n   `2.3 × 10⁹` nodes with intervals `[6, 7]` and `[7, 8]` (floor `ω − 1`,\n   upper bound the layout); the median refutation at `n = 120` is `10⁴–10⁵`\n   nodes and the 99th percentile `1.7 × 10⁶`. Chou et al.'s algorithm is\n   quadratic. The 3,448 record values cost nothing here because the\n   customer search had certified them at `n ≤ 75` in the ensemble campaign.\n   In the extension, `CT(6, 2, 2)` (666 vertices) was censored at\n   `1.4 × 10⁸` nodes with upper bound 10, which is `C(6, 2, 2)`.\nEOF\necho appended; tail -3 reports/scratch/forest_regime/run_ct_ext.log; ls reports/scratch/forest_regime | grep -E \"ext_values|rerun_censored.log\"; uptime","description":"Append the §3 header, method and findings to the report; poll the runs"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_017WJWq5KVpdgtu12SbNbq93":{"cwd":"/home/al/dev/MOSP"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017WJWq5KVpdgtu12SbNbq93","type":"tool_result","content":"appended\nCT(6,1,1) n=36 m=7: pw=7 refutation nodes=74 0.0s | pw(B)=2 refutation | wall 0.0s\nCT(6,2,2) n=666 m=133: pw=10 upper nodes=137186572 600.033s | pw(B)=3 minor | wall 604.3s\nCT(7,2,1) n=301 m=50: pw=11 minor nodes=4358202 4.929s | pw(B)=3 minor | wall 5.4s\n 00:40:22 up 12 days, 12:44,  1 user,  load average: 53.40, 45.09, 27.80","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"44e8c6d7-9d93-4ce6-b6cb-7af364193448","uuid":"6fb68558-4c75-4eb3-86dd-b66be4270ece","timestamp":"2026-11-01T00:40:22.570Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"appended\nCT(6,1,1) n=36 m=7: pw=7 refutation nodes=74 0.0s | pw(B)=2 refutation | wall 0.0s\nCT(6,2,2) n=666 m=133: pw=10 upper nodes=137186572 600.033s | pw(B)=3 minor | wall 604.3s\nCT(7,2,1) n=301 m=50: pw=11 minor nodes=4358202 4.929s | pw(B)=3 minor | wall 5.4s\n 00:40:22 up 12 days, 12:44,  1 user,  load average: 53.40, 45.09, 27.80","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/reports/excess_phase_diagram.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":1278,"oldLines":3,"newStart":1278,"newLines":171,"lines":[" | ensemble | ens_b_n100_m25_p0.1_i006  | 100 |  25 | 2.43 |        42 |     53 |         11 |"," | ensemble | ens_b_n100_m25_p0.12_i008 | 100 |  25 | 2.64 |        46 |     57 |         11 |"," ","+","+## 3. The forest regime, by brute force (item 03, 2026-11-01)","+","+**Question.** A, first half. When the incidence graph `B` is a forest the","+MOSP graph is a block graph whose blocks are the pattern cliques. What is its","+pathwidth as a function of the largest pattern `ω` and the shape of the","+forest: additive in `ω` and a depth, or multiplicative?","+","+**What was done.** Exact pathwidths by the C program","+`pathwidth_solver/cli/pathwidth` (graph6 on stdin, `-t 600 -l -v`, `nice -n","+19`, 6 workers while the one-minute load was below 24), every layout checked","+against the printed width by an independent vertex-separation routine","+(`learning.block_trees.vertex_separation`) and kept as the witness of the","+upper bound; nothing through the Python solvers, nothing written to","+`solutions/`. Four families:","+","+1. **Clique trees `CT(s, h, b)`** (`learning.block_trees.clique_tree`): one","+   root clique `K_s`; every customer at depth `d < h` is the hub of `b` new","+   cliques of size `s`, each adding `s − 1` customers at depth `d + 1`;","+   `n = s · Σ_{d ≤ h} (b (s − 1))^d`. `CT(2, h, b)` is a tree in which every","+   customer has `b` children (the root pair adjacent): `CT(2, h, 1)` a path,","+   `CT(2, h, 3)` two complete ternary trees of height `h` joined at their","+   roots, the tree `branch_lemma` bites on at every level. The item's grid is","+   `s ∈ {2, 3, 4, 5}`, `h ∈ {1, 2, 3}`, `b ∈ {1, 2, 3}`, `n ≤ 400`: 29 of the","+   36 cells. An **extension beyond the item's range** was added because the","+   grid proved in seconds: `s ≤ 8`, `h ≤ 4`, `400 < n ≤ 1000` (or `s > 5`,","+   `n ≤ 400`), 18 more cells; it is reported apart and the kill verdict is","+   decided on the grid alone.","+2. **The caterpillar of cliques** (`caterpillar_of_cliques`): a chain of","+   `L` cliques `K_s`, consecutive ones sharing one customer, with a pendant","+   `K_s` at every non-shared customer; `s ∈ {2, 3, 4, 5}`,","+   `L ∈ {1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 12}`, 24 graphs, `n ≤ 201`.","+3. **Random block trees** (`random_block_tree`): one pattern, then patterns","+   of random size — Chu & Stuckey's size law, `Binomial(n, d / n)` with 0 and","+   1 moved up to 2 (`REPORT_generator.md` §1.2) — attached at a uniformly","+   random existing customer until `n` customers; `d ∈ {2, 3, 4}`,","+   `n ∈ {20, 40, 60, 80, 100, 120}`, 50 per cell, 900 instances, every one","+   regenerable from `(n, d, seed)` with `seed = 100000 d + 100 n + i`. For a","+   connected incidence forest `e = 1 − 1/n` by the identity `γ / n = e − 1 +","+   κ / n`, so these sit at `e = 0.95–0.99`.","+4. **The forest instances on record** (item 01's map, `γ = 0`): 2 corpus and","+   3,446 ensemble instances with certified optima, `pw = optimum − 1`, nothing","+   solved; `n = 10–75`, `e = 0–0.98`, `κ` median 9 (they are mostly small","+   components).","+","+For every instance: `ω`, the block height `h` (the radius of the pattern","+adjacency forest in pattern steps, `h` for `CT(s, h, b)`), `pw(B)` by the C","+program on the incidence forest, and the candidates of the item, each a","+function of the forest: `ω − 1`, `ω − 1 + pw(B)`, `ω − 1 + h`,","+`ω (pw(B) + 1) − 1` (the blow-up of a path decomposition of `B`),","+`ω (tb(n + m) + 1) − 1` with `tb(N)` the least `k` with `N ≤ (5·3^k − 3)/2`","+(`TreeBound.lean`), and one of ours, `max(ω − 1, ω − 2 + pw(B))`, which is","+exact on every caterpillar of cliques. A would-be upper bound is scored by","+its violations (candidate below the exact value), its exact share and its","+slack.","+","+**Two independent checks of the C program.** (i) `learning/chou_block.py`","+implements the characterisation of Chou, Ko, Ho & Chen (2008; finding 1","+below) as a recursion memoised on vertex sets — exact, but exponential in","+the number of modified blocks as written, so usable to about 30 vertices;","+on every clique tree and caterpillar with `n ≤ 30` and 300 random block","+trees at `n = 8–24`, `d ∈ {2, 3, 4, 6}`, **328 of 328 agree with the C","+program** (`cross_check.log`). (ii) The one incidence tree the C program","+left censored (`B` of `CT(4, 2, 3)`, 485 vertices, upper bound 5 at 600 s)","+is settled by the complete-ternary-subtree lower bound","+(`ternary_subtree_height`; `pathwidth_ternaryTree` of `TreeBound.lean` plus","+monotonicity under subgraphs): it contains the complete ternary tree of","+height 5, so `pw(B) = 5` exactly.","+","+**Size range.** Clique trees `n = 4–365` (grid), `36–936` (extension);","+caterpillars `n = 4–201`; random bloc